# Get Started

Wyrd is pre-release. There is no published PyPI package yet, so you install from
a repo checkout and run through [`mise`](https://mise.jdx.dev/). This page gets
the local DataCard workflow running; the [first-card tutorial](/tutorials/first-card/)
explains what each step does.

<Aside title="What you need" data-variant="wyrd">

Python 3.10+, `git`, and `mise`. No running server is required for this
example — `DataCard.save()` writes local files and does not register anything.

</Aside>

## 1. Clone and set up

```bash
git clone https://github.com/mitari-ai/wyrd.git
cd wyrd
mise run py:setup
```

`py:setup` builds the Python environment and installs the local `wyrd` extension
(including pandas). This is the only supported install path until `py-wyrd`
publishes.

## 2. Run the DataCard example

```bash
mise run examples:python:datacard
```

Expected output (deterministic):

```text
saved_card_json: true
saved_data_file: data/data.parquet
loaded_rows: 3
loaded_columns: customer_id,churned,segment
labels: domain=customer,stage=example
annotation_source: examples/python/datacard_local_workflow.py
```

The example builds a `DataCard` from a pandas DataFrame, writes the local card
layout, loads it back, and prints what it read. The public import surface is:

```python
from wyrd.data import DataCard, PandasInterface
```

